  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Which of the following numbers are rational?\n\nI. $1.353124…$\n\nII. $1.5\\overset{―}{21}$\n\nIII. $4.055$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of our numbers in turn.\n\n- $1.353124…$ is not a rational number. The notation \"$…$\" tells us that digits after the decimal point extend forever. However, there are no repeating digits.\n\n- $1.5\\overset{―}{21}$ is a rational number. The notation $\\overset{―}{21}$ tells us that the digits $2$ and $1$ after the decimal point repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n1.5\\overset{―}{21}=1.52121212121…\n$$\n\nAny decimal with repeating digits can be represented as a ratio of two integers. In this case,\n\n$$\n1.5\\overset{―}{21}=\\frac{1\\,506}{990}.\n$$\n\n- $4.055$ is a rational number because it can be expressed as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n4.055=\\frac{4\\,055}{1\\,000}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
